chief washington correspondent that's  major garrett joins us now  major let's pick up where uh ben just  left where he said for the first time  if he stays on script and sometimes the  president does not do that  that he's going to mention  president-elect joe biden by name he has  shattered so many norms so many things  that we take  uh that we value that we honor what does  it mean that he  has not honored the tradition of of  of the handoff so your successor what is  the common thread in what you just  described gail  the self-generated drama of donald trump  we're all hanging around to see if he  will do something we know he should have  done weeks and months ago and every one  of his  outgoing president presidential  predecessors did without any  worry or concern at all he is built up  and created a false drama about whether  he's going to say the thing  that is so obvious that is so clear-cut  and is so deeply a part of our ingrained  traditions of a transition of power  it is an act of extravagant selfishness  you can't describe it as anything other  than that  yes he's entitled to it he retains the  powers of the presidency  but this is a moment and politics is an  enterprise where you have to do things  you don't want to  sometimes in human life we respect  people who we know  gosh if i was there it'd be really hard  for me to do that but grace matters  matters and history records it as such  so this is  a a day of the president's own creation  the drama built up around it  i think the question that comes to mind  is is this a character flaw as some  republicans have come to  determine or is it something strategic  that the president wants  for attention for attention's sake it is  a character reality  let me go back to a greek philosopher  you might have heard of heraclitus  a man's character is his fate the  smallness of this moment i talked to so  many trump  supporters people really worked inside  the white house really close this  campaign last night  they were unbelievably sad about this  moment how small it is  yeah how it could have been about a  transition of power and a recognition of  accomplishments  all that is so diminished because of the  president's own  actions he has diminished the exit of  his presidency  he could be large and gracious and talk  about the things he has done which are  not insignificant  but all of that is lost because of this  false hyper-generated drama  about nothing major he's leaving the  white house  here in a moment but he is still going  to be president in washington the  subject of an impeachment trial yes  mitch mcconnell on the floor of the  senate yesterday blamed him  again directly for the assault on the  capitol what did you make of those  remarks it was an extraordinary moment  you hear mitch mcconnell so there are a  lot of people in town who consider mitch  mcconnell kind of a cynical inside  player  very mechanistic but there are moments  when he protects the institution of  which he  serves and loves he is an  institutionalist whether you regard him  as a positive or negative one i'll leave  that to the partisans to fight out  but what i heard in those remarks were  mr president you launched an  attack on my home the legislative branch  of our government  the first branch of our government  outlined in the constitution i will not  forget it and is a very good chance i  won't  forgive it and the instrument of my not  forgiving you  is laying before this body in the next  couple of days it's called impeachment  isn't he sending others a message to  that you know what  maybe we should vote well he is  certainly giving them  ample room to have an open mind about  the underlying evidence and he's already  said  yes the president incited mobs the mob  was fed lies  by the president i was so struck by the  fact he did this  on the president's last full day in  office yes he would have said nothing he  could have said nothing  and i think for the senate majority  leader the relationship with the  president has always been strained but  the  majority leaders always said look i've  given you lots of room  lots of latitude to do your presidency  in your particularly  combative way what does it say i've  lived with that but i can't live with  this i can't abide this and i  won't let it go mitch mcconnell's very  carefully chosen words on his last day  as majority leader  do raise the question and we'll put this  to reince priebus later the president's  former chief of staff  of whether there is a permanent fracture  underway  in the republican party we heard someone  yesterday in a piece from manny  bojorquez refer to themselves as a  trumplikin  sure is that the future it might be in  the remarks the president's about to  give he will talk about the movement and  that it's in its earliest days  and it's going to continue and grow  around what  is it going to grow around the toxic  lies  of the last days of his presidency which  can only be described as a  assault not only on democracy the  capital but on facts and truth  and this reluctance to embrace the idea  that there's a transition of power and  someone new has been elected president  is that what your new party is about is  that your definition of patriotism if  you're going to call the patriot party  that's going to be a tough sell that is  not an enduring american political  message  if it gets closer to aggressiveness on  immigration  trade and things like that maybe it can  last but republicans are going to have  to make a decision about what they stand  for conspiracy theories  lies and anti-constitutionalist behavior  or something that's much more common in  the republican party oh front or about  160 years  i thought it was interesting too when  mitch mcconnell said not only lies by  the president but lies  by people powerful people people in  power  i thought he was sending messages there  as well yes you can read between the  lines ted cruz josh  so this moment as we await the  president's departure from the white  house and we are on the day when joe  biden and kamala harris will be  inaugurated it really underscores the  degree to which this is a break in what  is normal in washington on a typical  inauguration day we wouldn't have  multiple cameras and far-flung  correspondents we would have a single  shot  right of the incoming and outgoing  president exchanging gifts and  pleasantries on the white house steps  right and it doesn't mean that we have  to assume that everything is pleasant  and everyone loves each other that's not  the point  the point is in america we have  a lot of campaigning and we say lots of  tough things about one another but when  the voters  have made their verdict clear we don't  come apart we come together even if it's  ever so briefly and even if it's just  for ceremony  we do it to show the world and to show  ourselves  that these underlying traditions not  only matter in a historic sense  but they matter in a future sense  repeating them  inculcates us in very good behavior  that's  central not to our political futures not  to our political parties  but to the country itself valerie  jarrett made that point yesterday when  she joined us at cbs this morning she  said it was a difficult time for the  obamas right  to greet the trumps at the white house  that day because michelle obama's  written about this in her book  she believes that president trump's  rhetoric has actually put her family in  danger  but yet she said they chose grace at  that particular time right and if you go  back and look at president trump's  inaugural address  the very beginning of it he turns and  says i want to say how magnificent  president obama and first lady michelle  have been to us the transition has been  excellent  you have been magnificent big round of  applause but then in that speech we  heard the phrase  american carnage but still but the point  of that what i just said is that they  acknowledged one another as difficult as  it was  and this seamless garment of american  democracy was visible  it is rent this morning it will be torn  and the president is tearing it and  he's not going to be there and what that  sets up i mean to the point you're  making now major this  this is joe biden's day the focus should  be on joe biden  and we're spending time talking about  the fact that donald trump won't go to  the inauguration well you know what we  can try  maybe we should not do that we shouldn't  do that we can turn the hourglass over  right now  from where i begin this artificial drama  which keeps the president at the center  of it that's the common thread

